<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/15th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Lipany</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1434">1434</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussite-wars">Hussite Wars</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Lipany was fought east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> on 30 May 1434 near the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussite-wars">Hussite Wars</a>. An alliance of moderate Utraquists and Catholics defeated the radical Taborite and Orphan field armies, and their leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prokop-the-great">Prokop the Great</a> was killed. The battle ended radical Hussite power and opened the way to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/compacts-of-basel">Compacts of Basel</a> and the recognition of Sigismund as king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-lipany.jpg" alt="Battle of Lipany" width="320" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Luděk Marold · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skupina_bratrsk%C3%BDch_vojev%C5%AFdc%C5%AF.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The battle, also called the Battle of Český Brod, was fought at Lipany, 40 km east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>. The army of the moderate Hussite nobility and the Catholics, the Bohemian League, faced the radical Taborites and Orphans, or Sirotci, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prokop-the-great">Prokop the Great</a> as overall commander and Jan Čapek of Sány as cavalry commander. The radicals set up a Wagenburg, a fortified ring of wagons, on a hill that gave them a strong position, and the two armies stood facing each other for some time. An attempt by the Utraquists to settle the conflict by negotiation failed because the two sides' positions could not be reconciled.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Three days after the failed talks the Leaguers advanced on the radicals' camp. An exchange of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> fire did little harm at that distance, and then, to the radicals' surprise, the Leaguers began to retreat with all their wagons. Taking this for flight, the radical commanders opened the Wagenburg and attacked. The retreat was a trick: the Leaguers halted and opened fire from their wagons, while their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a>, hidden near the radical camp, struck from the side and broke into the open Wagenburg.</p><p>The radical army quickly collapsed, and Čapek of Sány fled with all his men to the nearby town of Kolín. The battle became a massacre of the lightly equipped radical forces. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prokop-the-great">Prokop the Great</a> and Prokop the Lesser were both killed in a last stand at the wagons. Jan Roháč of Dubá and other leaders were captured, and about 700 ordinary soldiers who surrendered on promises of renewed military service were burned to death in nearby barns.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Taborite army was much weakened, and the Orphans virtually ceased to exist as a military force. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/compacts-of-basel">Compacts of Basel</a> were signed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihlava">Jihlava</a> on 5 July 1436, and the next month all major factions accepted Sigismund as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-of-bohemia">King of Bohemia</a>. Sigismund remarked that the Bohemians could be overcome only by Bohemians. The last Taborite force, under Roháč, was besieged in his castle Sion near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutna-hora">Kutná Hora</a>; when it fell, Roháč, still refusing to accept Sigismund as king, was hanged in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> on 9 September 1437.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Lipany” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their defeat came from within, when an alliance of moderate Utraquists and Catholics destroyed the radical field armies at the <b>Battle of Lipany</b> in 1434.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Kingdom of Bohemia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Calixtines united with the Roman Catholics and destroyed them at the <b>Battle of Lipany</b> on 30 May 1434.</blockquote><a href="/movement/hussites">Hussites · Outcomes</a></li></ul></section>
